<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1801">1801</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The United <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain</a> and Ireland was the state created on 1 January 1801 by the Acts of Union, which merged the Kingdom of Great Britain and the Kingdom of Ireland under one parliament at Westminster. It ruled the largest empire of the 19th century. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-war-of-independence">Irish War of Independence</a> the Irish Free State left in December 1922, and the remaining state became the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland.jpg" alt="United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Acts of Union 1800 Vector: Yaddah · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Kingdom_(3-5).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Kingdom of Ireland had enjoyed a brief period of limited independence, which ended after the Irish Rebellion of 1798, during the war with revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Fear that an independent Ireland might side with France led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain</a> to decide on union, passed by the parliaments of both kingdoms and in effect from 1 January 1801. The Irish had been led to believe that the loss of their legislature would be offset by Catholic emancipation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> held that it would violate his coronation oath to uphold the Protestant faith, and his refusal led the prime minister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-younger">William Pitt the Younger</a>, to resign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> was at war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> almost until 1815. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s plans to invade failed, chiefly because of his weaker navy, and a fleet under Nelson defeated the French and Spanish navies at Trafalgar in 1805. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> ended in a stalemate and a negotiated peace at the end of 1814 that restored the prewar boundaries. From the defeat of Napoleon to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, Britain was almost continuously at peace with the other Great Powers, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> supported what was later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pax-britannica">Pax Britannica</a>. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-exhibition">Great Exhibition</a> of 1851 it was described as the workshop of the world, and it exercised influence through free trade and investment as well as through a formal empire.</p><p>Catholic emancipation came in 1829 after a campaign by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daniel-oconnell">Daniel O'Connell</a>, and the Test Acts were repealed in 1828. Reform legislation followed in 1832, 1867 and 1884. The Great Irish Famine of the mid-19th century, worsened by government inaction, led to a collapse in population in much of Ireland. Queen Victoria reigned from 1837 to 1901. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> followed in 1914, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, whose neutrality <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> had guaranteed, and it ended in an Allied victory in 1918 at a massive cost.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-rising">Easter Rising</a> of 1916 was suppressed after a week, and the execution of 15 leaders alienated nationalist opinion and moved it from home rule toward independence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-war-of-independence">Irish War of Independence</a> ended in British recognition of the Irish Free State in 1922. As a dominion, the Free State was no longer part of the United Kingdom and ceased to be represented at Westminster. Six north-eastern counties, which had been governed under home rule since 1920, stayed in the Union. Self-governing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> had also grown out of the empire. The present United Kingdom is a direct continuation of the state that remained.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 1 January 1801 Great Britain and Ireland became the <b>United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland</b>, with Irish members sitting at Westminster.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1 January 1801 Ireland became part of the <b>United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland</b>, sending members to Westminster, and the Irish Parliament ceased to exist; Catholic emancipation, promised informally, was delayed until 1829.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The country's full name changed only in 1927: from 1922 to 1927 it was still officially the <b>United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
